{"subjectId":"sql","id":"lag-lead","title":"LAG, LEAD & Value Functions","group":"Subqueries, CTEs & Windows","order":38,"html":"<p class=\"lead\"><code>LAG</code> and <code>LEAD</code> read a value from a previous or following row of the same window — day-over-day changes, gaps between events and “next step” analysis without a self join.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT day, amount,\n       LAG(amount)       OVER (ORDER BY day) AS prev_day,\n       LEAD(amount)      OVER (ORDER BY day) AS next_day,\n       amount - LAG(amount) OVER (ORDER BY day) AS change,\n       LAG(amount, 2, 0) OVER (ORDER BY day) AS two_days_ago\nFROM daily_sales\nORDER BY day;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>day        | amount | prev_day | next_day | change | two_days_ago\n-----------+--------+----------+----------+--------+-------------\n2024-03-01 | 120    | NULL     | 80       | NULL   | 0\n2024-03-02 | 80     | 120      | 150      | -40    | 0\n2024-03-03 | 150    | 80       | 90       | 70     | 120\n2024-03-04 | 90     | 150      | 200      | -60    | 80\n2024-03-05 | 200    | 90       | 60       | 110    | 150\n2024-03-06 | 60     | 200      | NULL     | -140   | 90</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Signature: <code>LAG(expr, offset = 1, default = NULL)</code>. The first row has no previous row, so it gets the default.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-trending-up\"></i>Growth in percent</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>WITH monthly AS (\n    SELECT strftime('%Y-%m', o.order_date) AS month,\n           SUM(oi.quantity * oi.unit_price) AS revenue\n    FROM orders o JOIN order_items oi ON oi.order_id = o.order_id\n    WHERE o.status &lt;&gt; 'cancelled'\n    GROUP BY month\n)\nSELECT month, revenue,\n       LAG(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY month) AS prev,\n       ROUND(100.0 * (revenue - LAG(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY month))\n             / LAG(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY month), 1) AS growth_pct\nFROM monthly\nORDER BY month;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>month   | revenue | prev  | growth_pct\n--------+---------+-------+-----------\n2024-01 | 66700   | NULL  | NULL\n2024-02 | 12900   | 66700 | -80.7\n2024-03 | 63700   | 12900 | 393.8</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-clock\"></i>Per group: time between a customer's orders</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT customer_id, order_id, order_date,\n       LAG(order_date) OVER w AS previous_order,\n       julianday(order_date) - julianday(LAG(order_date) OVER w) AS days_since\nFROM orders\nWINDOW w AS (PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY order_date)\nORDER BY customer_id, order_date;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>customer_id | order_id | order_date | previous_order | days_since\n------------+----------+------------+----------------+-----------\n1           | 101      | 2024-01-05 | NULL           | NULL\n1           | 103      | 2024-02-03 | 2024-01-05     | 29.0\n1           | 108      | 2024-03-15 | 2024-02-03     | 41.0\n2           | 102      | 2024-01-12 | NULL           | NULL\n2           | 106      | 2024-03-01 | 2024-01-12     | 49.0\n3           | 104      | 2024-02-14 | NULL           | NULL\n3           | 107      | 2024-03-08 | 2024-02-14     | 23.0\n4           | 105      | 2024-02-20 | NULL           | NULL</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p><code>WINDOW w AS (…)</code> names a window once and reuses it (standard SQL; PostgreSQL, MySQL 8, SQLite — not SQL Server before 2022).</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrow-bar-to-left\"></i>FIRST_VALUE, LAST_VALUE, NTH_VALUE</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT name, dept_id, salary,\n       FIRST_VALUE(name) OVER (PARTITION BY dept_id ORDER BY salary DESC) AS top_earner,\n       LAST_VALUE(name)  OVER (PARTITION BY dept_id ORDER BY salary DESC) AS last_value_default,\n       LAST_VALUE(name)  OVER (PARTITION BY dept_id ORDER BY salary DESC\n                               ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND UNBOUNDED FOLLOWING) AS lowest_earner\nFROM employees\nWHERE dept_id = 2\nORDER BY salary DESC;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>name  | dept_id | salary | top_earner | last_value_default | lowest_earner\n------+---------+--------+------------+--------------------+--------------\nPriya | 2       | 110000 | Priya      | Priya              | Arjun\nNeha  | 2       | 72000  | Priya      | Neha               | Arjun\nArjun | 2       | 70000  | Priya      | Arjun              | Arjun</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p><code>LAST_VALUE</code> with the default frame returns the <strong>current</strong> row (the frame ends at the current row), not the last row of the partition. Specify <code>ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND UNBOUNDED FOLLOWING</code>, or use <code>FIRST_VALUE</code> with the opposite sort.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How do you compute the difference between the current row and the previous row?</summary><div><p><code>value - LAG(value) OVER (PARTITION BY group ORDER BY time)</code>. Before window functions this needed a self join on “the previous row”, which is slower and harder to write.</p></div></details></section>"}